  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protection of workers from unacceptable forms of work through the promotion of fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Country Programme Outcome will continue to promote the ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, adopted in 1998 and amended in 2022, is an expression of commitment by governments, employers&apos; and workers&apos; organizations to uphold basic human values - values that are vital to our social and economic lives. It affirms the obligations and commitments that are inherent in membership of the ILO, namely: freedom of association and the effective recognition of the right to collective bargaining, the elimination of all forms of forced or compulsory labour the effective abolition of child labour, the elimination of discrimination in respect of employment and occupation and a safe and healthy working environment. 

The nature and scope of the problem is reflected at the national level in the Republic of Zambia. Among the Children in paid employment, the sectors Agriculture, Forestry and Fishing while the mining and quarrying also have incidences of child labour. Nevertheless, the number of children engaging in mining activities has also increased markedly in recent years. A baseline study conducted in the Copperbelt and Central provinces found children surveyed were involved in mining, in roles such as digging, transporting, carrying rocks, crushing rocks, loading, selling and sorting minerals. Most children working on mine sites are male, but girls also work in mining areas, often providing domestic services such as cooking, childcare, and selling of foodstuffs. Likewise, an ILO informal site visits to legal and illegal mines in Chingola (Copperbelt) and Mumbwa (Central) found evidence of children working on mine sites digging copper and extracting scrap metal. In some areas, children are employed by illegal mining syndicates called jerabo gangs, but many also work alongside their parents to support their families. The high level of informality in the Artisanal and Small-Scale Mining (ASM) sector, where workers in the mining and quarrying sector are</narrative>
  </description>
